 1 big thing: FDA OKs groundbreaking cancer drug 
 
 
 &nbsp; 
 
 Illustration: Maura Kearns/Axios The FDA approved a groundbreaking new drug for the most common form of pancreatic cancer, offering patients a more effective treatment against one of the deadliest types of cancer.

 💊 The first-of-a-kind pill, called daraxonrasib, blocks a mutated protein that fuels tumor growth in more than 90% of pancreatic cancer cases. 

 The daily pill will be sold by Revolution Medicines under the brand name Rasonque. Patients taking the drug nearly doubled their survival time, with fewer severe side effects, in a study that randomly assigned them to receive the experimental treatment or additional chemotherapy. 

 The treatment hit the spotlight earlier this year when former Sen. Ben Sasse (R-Neb.) described his positive experience with it on "60 Minutes." 🩻 Pancreatic cancer is one of the deadliest cancers, in large part because it's often not detected until it's spread.

 The American Cancer Society estimates that about 67,000 new cases will be diagnosed in the U.S. this year, while more than 52,000 people will die from the disease. Dr. Pashtoon Kasi of City of Hope Orange County, a California-based cancer center, tells AP: "This is not a cure, it's one more option for these patients."

 "But it's the best option we've ever had." Go deeper . 

 
 
